course: dictionary
lesson: 14547
title: Old words for deadly sickness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a hard word from the words around it. «Long ago, a pestilence swept through the town, and many families lost someone they loved.» The clues are swept through the town and lost someone, so a pestilence must be something deadly that spreads.
explain: Pestilence is an old, formal word for a deadly disease that spreads quickly through many people. «In history lessons we learn that the Black Death killed huge numbers of people in Europe, and it was a pestilence.» Check by swapping in disease: the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: In the Bible, pestilence is one of the disasters sent as punishment or warning, like the plagues of Egypt. It often goes together with famine and war. Old writers also used it for anything very harmful or annoying: «That buzzing wasp was a pestilence at our picnic.»
fact: Pestilence means a deadly disease that spreads quickly through many people.
fact: Pestilence is an old or formal word; today we often say epidemic or pandemic.
fact: In the Bible, pestilence is one of the disasters sent as punishment or warning.
fact: «Pestilence» was used for the Black Death of 1347 to 1351, which killed huge numbers of people in Europe.
fact: Pestilence is a noun: one pestilence, many pestilences.
exercise: «Long ago, a pestilence swept through the town.» What is a pestilence? | A type of fast horse | *A deadly disease that spreads quickly through many people | A kind of old castle
exercise: In the Bible, pestilence is sent together with famine and war. What is it sent as? | *A punishment or warning | A gift to the people | A new road
exercise: Which word do we often use today for a disease that spreads quickly through many people? | Pendulum | *Epidemic | Pedestal
test: «In 1347 the Black Death, a terrible pestilence, spread across Europe.» What was the Black Death? | *A pestilence that killed huge numbers of people in Europe | A famous old ship | A kind of food
test: «The noisy neighbour's dog was a pestilence on the whole street.» In this sentence, pestilence means | A new road | A deadly disease | *Something very annoying
exam: Which of these describes pestilence as the Bible uses it? | *A disaster sent as punishment or warning | A kind of sunny weather | A friendly meeting
